Production Installation Guide: Automated Apache Tika Server in WSL
This document outlines the procedure for configuring a persistent Apache Tika server on a specific WSL2 instance. It has been tailored for the target system detailed below.

System Profile
Windows Hostname: DESKTOP-B50D2C2
Windows IP Address: 10.159.107.164
Host OS: Windows 10 Enterprise (2009)
WSL Instance: Ubuntu 24.04.2 LTS
Docker Version: 28.2.2
1. Objective
To configure a Tika server that starts automatically on WSL launch, managed by the systemd init system for maximum reliability. The service will be accessible from other machines on the local domain network.

2. Prerequisites
A WSL2 instance running Ubuntu 24.04.2 LTS.
The user has sudo (administrator) privileges within the Ubuntu instance.
docker.io has been installed via sudo apt-get install docker.io.
Access to PowerShell as an Administrator on the Windows host for firewall verification.
3. Configuration Steps
Step 3.1: Enable systemd in WSL
This ensures that systemd is the init system, which is required to manage services at boot.

Create or overwrite the WSL configuration file.
Bash

sudo tee /etc/wsl.conf <<EOF
[boot]
systemd = true
EOF
From PowerShell, fully shut down WSL for the change to take effect.
PowerShell

wsl --shutdown
Relaunch your WSL instance.
Step 3.2: Configure and Enable the Docker Service
This step resolves a common conflict between Docker and systemd in WSL.

Fix the Docker daemon.json configuration by overwriting the file.
Bash

sudo tee /etc/docker/daemon.json <<EOF
{
  "iptables": false,
  "log-driver": "json-file",
  "log-opts": {
    "max-size": "10m",
    "max-file": "3"
  }
}
EOF
Reload the systemd configuration and enable the Docker service to start on boot.
Bash

sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable docker.service
Step 3.3: Pull the Tika Docker Image
Explicitly pull the Tika image before creating a service for it. This makes troubleshooting easier.

Bash

sudo docker pull apache/tika:latest-full
(Optional but Recommended): For true production stability, find the unique image digest to ensure you always run this exact version.

Bash

# This command will output the unique digest, copy it for the next step.
docker inspect apache/tika:latest-full --format '{{index .RepoDigests 0}}'
Step 3.4: Create the Tika systemd Service
This service file tells systemd how to manage the Tika container, ensuring it starts only after Docker is ready.

Create a new service file for Tika.

Bash

sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/tika.service
Add the following service definition. Note the verified paths and resource limits.

Note: Replace apache/tika:latest-full with the unique digest from the previous step for maximum stability. It will look something like apache/tika@sha256:abcd...

Ini, TOML

[Unit]
Description=Apache Tika Server Container
Requires=docker.service
After=docker.service

[Service]
Restart=always
ExecStartPre=-/usr/bin/docker stop tika-server
ExecStartPre=-/usr/bin/docker rm tika-server
ExecStart=/usr/bin/docker run --name tika-server -p 9998:9998 --memory=2g --cpus=1 apache/tika:latest-full
ExecStop=/usr/bin/docker stop tika-server

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
Save the file and exit nano (Ctrl+X, Y, Enter).

Step 3.5: Enable and Start Services
Enable your new Tika service and start both services for the current session.

Bash

sudo systemctl enable tika.service
sudo systemctl start docker.service
sudo systemctl start tika.service
4. Verification and Connection
Step 4.1: Verify Service Status
Check that both Docker and Tika services are running correctly inside WSL.

Bash

# Check the systemd services. Both should show "active (running)" in green.
sudo systemctl status docker.service tika.service

# Check that the container is running via Docker.
sudo docker ps
You should see the tika-server container listed.

Step 4.2: Verify Windows Firewall Access
This step is critical to ensure your server is accessible from the network.

Open PowerShell as an Administrator on the Windows 10 host.
Run the following command to check for a firewall rule on port 9998.
PowerShell

Get-NetFirewallPortFilter | ? {$_.LocalPort -eq 9998} | Get-NetFirewallRule | select DisplayName, Enabled, Profile, Direction, Action
Analyze the Output:
Ideal Result: You will see a rule that is Enabled: True for the Profile: Domain.
If No Rule is Found: WSL may have failed to create the rule. Create it manually with this PowerShell command:
PowerShell

New-NetFirewallRule -DisplayName "Allow Tika Server (WSL)" -Direction Inbound -Action Allow -Protocol TCP -LocalPort 9998 -Profile Domain
Step 4.3: Final Connection Test
The Tika server is now automated. You can access it from any machine on your domain network using the Windows host's name or IP address.

URL: http://DESKTOP-B50D2C2:9998
Alternative URL: http://10.159.107.164:9998
You can test this by opening the URL in a web browser or using curl from any machine on the network. To confirm it's working, visit http://DESKTOP-B50D2C2:9998/version.
